  <div class="header-text">
    <h1>Field Companion</h1>
    <p>XP Deus 2 · finds, coverage &amp; reference</p>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- FIELD MAP -->
<div class="panel active" id="tab-map">
  <div class="card">
    <h2>Field map</h2>
    <p>Tracks where you've walked and, optionally, colours in signal strength where you've logged a reading. Works fully offline — no map tiles, just your own GPS trail plotted to scale.</p>
    <div class="row">
      <button class="primary" id="btnTrackToggle">Start walking</button>
      <button id="btnLogPoint">Log reading here</button>
    </div>
    <div class="row">
      <button class="danger" id="btnClearTrack">Clear track</button>
      <button id="btnExportTrack">Export CSV</button>
    </div>
    <p class="readout" id="trackStatus" style="margin-top:8px;">Not tracking · 0 points</p>
    <div class="row">
      <button id="btnMapMode">Switch to live map</button>
    </div>
    <div class="row" style="align-items:center;">
      <label style="margin:0; display:flex; align-items:center; gap:6px;"><input type="checkbox" id="chkLidar" style="width:auto; margin:0;"> LIDAR hillshade overlay</label>
    </div>
    <canvas id="mapCanvas" height="260"></canvas>
    <div id="leafletMap" style="display:none; height:260px; border-radius:5px; overflow:hidden; border:1px solid var(--hairline);"></div>
    <div class="legend">
      <span><span class="dot" style="background:#556677"></span>walked path</span>
      <span><span class="dot" style="background:#4C8BF5"></span>low signal</span>
      <span><span class="dot" style="background:#6E8F72"></span>mid</span>
      <span><span class="dot" style="background:#D9A35E"></span>high</span>
      <span><span class="dot" style="background:#C0392B"></span>very high / dig it</span>
    </div>
    <p class="small" style="margin-top:6px;">"Live map" shows real streets/terrain under your position, and the LIDAR overlay shades in subtle relief — hollows, banks, old field boundaries — from the Environment Agency's national survey. Both need a data or wifi signal to load; the offline sketch is always available with no signal at all.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="card">
    <label for="lastSignal">Signal / VDI to attach to next logged point (optional)</label>
    <input type="number" id="lastSignal" placeholder="e.g. 82">
    <p class="small">Tip: log a point every time you get a solid signal, whether you dig it or not — over a session this builds a real map of where the good ground is, not just where your finds were.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- FIND LOG -->
<div class="panel" id="tab-finds">
  <div class="card">
    <h2>Find log</h2>
    <input type="text" id="findName" placeholder="What was it? (e.g. Victorian penny)">
    <div class="row">
      <input type="number" id="findVDI" placeholder="VDI / target ID">
      <input type="number" id="findDepth" placeholder="Depth (cm)">
    </div>
    <textarea id="findNotes" placeholder="Notes — condition, exact spot, program used…" rows="2"></textarea>
    <div class="row">
      <button id="btnFindLoc">Use current location</button>
      <button class="primary" id="btnSaveFind">Save find</button>
    </div>
    <p class="readout small" id="findLocStatus">No location attached</p>
  </div>
  <div class="card">
    <div class="row" style="align-items:center;">
      <h2 style="margin:0;">History</h2>
      <button id="btnExportFinds" style="flex:none;">Export CSV</button>
    </div>
    <div id="findList"><p class="small">No finds logged yet.</p></div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- VDI HELPER -->
<div class="panel" id="tab-vdi">
  <div class="card">
    <h2>Reading the numbers</h2>
    <p>General target-ID behaviour on a 0–99 style scale. Every machine (and every field) shifts these a little, so treat this as a starting orientation, not gospel — the numbers that matter are the ones you personally dig and confirm on your own ground.</p>
    <table>
      <tr><th>Range</th><th>Usually</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
      <tr><td class="tier-ferrous">0–12</td><td>Iron, nails, steel</td><td>Ferrous — often a grunt/low tone rather than a clean bell</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="tier-low">13–29</td><td>Foil, small gold (rings), some pull tabs</td><td>Worth checking — small gold rings hide here</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="tier-low">30–39</td><td>Nickel, some pull tabs</td><td>Coin-sized nickel (e.g. 5p/10p-ish) can sit near here</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="tier-med">40–59</td><td>Pull tabs, aluminium, zinc cent-type</td><td>Classic "junk" band, but screw caps/some jewellery overlaps</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="tier-med">60–75</td><td>Copper-alloy coins, brass</td><td>Old pennies, bronze relics typically land here</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="tier-high">76–89</td><td>Clad/cupro-nickel coins</td><td>Modern 10p/50p-style coins</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="tier-high">90–99</td><td>Silver</td><td>High-conductivity — hammered and pre-1947 silver often peg here</td></tr>
    </table>
    <div class="note">Bottle caps and folded foil are the great impersonators — they can land right in the coin bands but usually give a broken, unstable tone across a few sweeps rather than the clean repeatable signal a coin gives lying flat.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="card">
    <h2>Log a calibration point</h2>
    <p class="small">Dug something? Log the ID and what it turned out to be — over time this becomes your own site-specific chart, which is worth more than any generic table.</p>
    <div class="row">
      <input type="number" id="vdiValue" placeholder="VDI value">
      <input type="text" id="vdiItem" placeholder="Turned out to be…">
    </div>
    <button class="primary" id="btnSaveVDI">Add to my chart</button>
    <canvas id="vdiCanvas" height="140" style="margin-top:10px;"></canvas>
    <div id="vdiList" style="margin-top:8px;"></div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- DEUS 2 INFO -->
<div class="panel" id="tab-deus">
  <div class="card">
    <h2>Settings, plainly</h2>
    <table>
      <tr><th>Setting</th><th>What it does</th><th>When to move it</th></tr>
      <tr><td>Sensitivity</td><td>How hard the coil listens</td><td>Turn down if you get random chatter with nothing there (mineralised ground, EMI); turn up on quiet, clean ground for extra depth</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Reactivity</td><td>How fast the machine reprocesses signals as you sweep</td><td>Higher in trashy ground to separate targets lying close together; lower for maximum depth on isolated, clean sites</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Discrimination</td><td>Rejects a band of low, iron-like readings</td><td>Keep low/near zero on old or hammered-coin sites — you lose faint deep silver if you push it too high just to silence iron</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Tones / tone breaks</td><td>Where the audio pitch changes across the ID scale</td><td>3–4 tones is plenty to learn a site by ear; full tones gives more detail but more noise</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Ground balance / tracking</td><td>Cancels the response of mineral in the soil itself</td><td>Re-balance whenever you move to noticeably different ground (e.g. field to beach, clay to chalk)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Silencer</td><td>Smooths out rusty/broken chatter</td><td>Nudge up if iron is "spitting" through as broken high tones, but it can shave a little depth</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Iron volume</td><td>How loud rejected iron sounds</td><td>Turn down when you're sick of nails on a known trashy field; keep some volume on new ground — iron grunts can mask a good target underneath</td></tr>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="card">
    <h3>Stock programs, explained</h3>
    <p class="small">Exact names/order shift a little between firmware versions — check your remote's program list against these, but the intent behind each stays the same.</p>

    <h3 style="margin-top:14px;">General</h3>
    <p>The factory default and the right starting point on any ground you don't know yet. It runs a balanced multi-frequency mix so it isn't specialised for depth, small gold, or iron-heavy land — it's the one to load first on a new permission, get a feel for the ground and the junk level, and only move off once you know what you're actually dealing with.</p>

    <h3>Sensitive</h3>
    <p>Pushes toward the higher end of the frequency range, which favours small and low-conductivity targets — thin gold rings, small hammered coins, tiny relics. Costs you a little stability in mineralised or electrically noisy ground in exchange for that small-target sensitivity, so it shines on quiet permission land more than on a busy iron-strewn site.</p>

    <h3>Sensitive FT</h3>
    <p>Same frequency bias as Sensitive, but running Full Tones audio — every conductivity band gets its own distinct pitch rather than being grouped into 3–5 tones. Gives you much more audio detail to learn from, at the cost of a noisier, busier listening experience; better suited to someone who already knows the ground and wants maximum information from every signal.</p>

    <h3>Deep / Deep HC</h3>
    <p>Biased toward lower frequencies and lower reactivity, which is what buys depth on larger, mid-to-high conductivity targets (bigger coins, cupro-nickel, larger relics). HC (high conductivity) leans even further that way. Trade-off is reduced sensitivity to small, low-conductivity targets — a good choice once you suspect a field has been hunted hard at normal depth and you want to see what's sitting deeper.</p>

    <h3>Relic</h3>
    <p>Built around frequency subtraction — comparing responses across frequencies to strip out the ground and iron signal, leaving larger, older, often lower-conductivity targets more visible underneath. Reactivity is usually kept low here, which favours depth over fast target separation, so it's suited to sparser, older sites rather than trashy modern land where lots of targets sit close together.</p>

    <h3>Beach / Beach Sens</h3>
    <p>Tuned to reject the fast, salt-driven false signals that wet sand produces, which a land program will struggle with. Beach Sens adds extra sensitivity to small non-ferrous targets (jewellery, small coins) on top of that salt rejection — the pair to use any time you're within reach of the tideline, wet sand, or salt-damp ground.</p>

    <h3>Gold Field</h3>
    <p>Prospecting-oriented: sensitivity is weighted toward the low-conductivity end of the scale where small gold nuggets sit, since nuggets often read close to iron or ground signal and get thrown away by normal discrimination. Expect more chatter in mineralised ground in exchange for not missing genuinely small, low-conductivity gold.</p>

    <h3>Diving</h3>
    <p>Configured for stability underwater and in saltwater specifically — resistant to the false signals salt water causes, and biased toward rings and coins over foil, which is the dominant junk category underwater. Not a land program; only relevant if you're detecting submerged.</p>

    <h3>Deus Mono</h3>
    <p>Runs a single frequency (rather than the usual multi-frequency blend) with an extended range and stronger EMI rejection. Worth switching to when multi-frequency operation itself becomes the problem — heavy electrical interference, or ground so mineralised that multi-frequency processing gets unstable — since a single clean frequency can outperform multi-frequency in exactly those conditions.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="card">
    <h3>Legacy 4.2</h3>
    <p>A free, community-built custom program (not a stock XP program) built around frequency management to pull old, low-conductivity targets — Saxon, medieval and Roman coins in particular — cleanly out of iron-heavy ground. Detectorists mainly reach for it on old pasture, deserted medieval village sites, and other historically "dirty" land where a stock program either buries the target in iron noise or discriminates it away entirely.</p>
    <p>Because it's a downloadable program rather than a menu option, install it through the official NoxEditor program installer (or by entering the program code on your remote, if you were given one) rather than trying to rebuild it from memory — exact tone-break and reactivity values get refined between releases, so the authoritative version is whatever the current install file gives you.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="card">
    <h3>Forager 4</h3>
    <p>Another free community program, built around "morphing" — it blends frequency behaviour as you sweep rather than sitting on one fixed profile. That makes it a forgiving general-purpose choice across mixed or changing ground (a field that goes from dry topsoil to a damp hollow, for instance), which is why people tend to reach for it as a do-it-all option on a new permission before settling into something more specialised.</p>
    <p>Same advice as Legacy: install via the official program file/code rather than hand-entering settings, so you get whatever the current tuned release actually is.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="note">Both are free downloads distributed through XP's dealer network and community channels (search "XP Deus 2 Legacy 4.2" / "Forager 4" for the current install code and video walkthrough) — worth re-checking before a big trip in case a newer revision has replaced the one you have.</div>
</div>

<!-- UK COINS -->
<div class="panel" id="tab-coins">
  <div class="card searchbox">
    <input type="text" id="coinSearch" placeholder="Search coins (e.g. sixpence, silver, 1967)">
  </div>
  <div class="card">
    <table id="coinTable">
      <thead><tr><th>Coin</th><th>Period</th><th>Composition</th><th>Size</th><th>Conductivity</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody id="coinBody"></tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="note">Conductivity tiers are rough guidance (ferrous / low / medium / high), not fixed VDI numbers — always confirm against your own machine. The one to remember: copper-plated steel 1p/2p (1992 onward) are magnetic and can read like ferrous rubbish despite looking exactly like the older bronze coins.</div>

  <div class="card">
    <h2>How this app stores your data</h2>
    <p>No cookies, and nothing is sent anywhere. Your track, finds, and VDI log are saved with a browser feature called <strong>localStorage</strong> — a small local database the page keeps entirely on your phone, tied to this file/address. There's no server behind this app at all; everything happens in the browser tab itself.</p>
    <p>Practical points worth knowing:</p>
    <ul class="small" style="padding-left:18px; line-height:1.6;">
      <li>It persists between visits — closing the tab or restarting your phone won't lose it.</li>
      <li>It's tied to <em>this exact file or web address</em>. If you move the file, re-host it at a new URL, or open it in a different browser, that counts as a different "origin" and starts with an empty store.</li>
      <li>"Clear browsing data" in your browser settings will wipe it, and on some phones (iOS Safari in particular) storage for infrequently-opened sites can be cleared automatically after a long period of inactivity.</li>
      <li>Because of that, use the backup tool below — it saves everything to an actual JSON file on your phone, independent of browser storage entirely.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h2>Backup &amp; restore (local JSON file)</h2>
    <p>This saves your entire track, finds log, and VDI log into one <code>.json</code> file on your phone — not tied to a browser, an origin, or this specific copy of the app. It's the safest way to carry your data between devices, hosting changes, or a fresh install.</p>
    <div style="display:flex; gap:10px; flex-wrap:wrap; margin-top:10px;">
      <button class="btn" id="backupExportBtn">Export backup (.json)</button>
      <button class="btn" id="backupImportBtn">Restore from backup</button>
      <input type="file" id="backupFileInput" accept="application/json,.json" style="display:none;">
    </div>
    <p class="small" style="margin-top:8px;">Exporting downloads a file to your <strong>Files/Downloads</strong> folder — move it into iCloud Drive or another folder you trust for safekeeping. Restoring reads a previously exported file and replaces the app's current track/finds/VDI data with what's in it, so only restore a backup you mean to load in full.</p>
    <p class="small" id="backupStatus"></p>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- OTHER FINDS ID GUIDE -->
<div class="panel" id="tab-other">
  <div class="card searchbox">
    <input type="text" id="otherSearch" placeholder="Search (e.g. button, musket ball, Roman)">
  </div>
  <div class="card">
    <table id="otherTable">
      <thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Period</th><th>Material</th><th>Signal</th><th>ID tips</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody id="otherBody"></tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="note">Lead and pewter (musket balls, toy figures, some buttons) are poor conductors and often read surprisingly low — close to iron on some machines. If you're hunting an old site with the discrimination turned up to silence junk, you may be silencing a whole category of genuine finds along with it.</div>

  <div class="card">
    <h2>Reporting what you find (England, Wales &amp; NI)</h2>
    <p>Under the Treasure Act 1996, an object counts as Treasure if it's <strong>at least 300 years old</strong> and either: it's not a coin and contains at least 10% gold or silver by weight; or it's part of a group of two or more coins of that age containing that much precious metal (or ten or more coins of any precious-metal content); or it's a group of two or more prehistoric base-metal objects found together. Anything found alongside a Treasure item counts as Treasure too, regardless of what it's made of.</p>
    <p>If you think you've found Treasure, you're legally required to report it to your local coroner — in practice, this means contacting your local Finds Liaison Officer (part of the Portable Antiquities Scheme), who handles the process for you. You have 14 days from realising it might qualify to report it.</p>
    <p>Scotland works differently — there's no 10%/300-year test; anything of possible archaeological or historical significance should be reported to the Treasure Trove Unit.</p>
    <p>Even when something isn't legally Treasure, recording it with your local Finds Liaison Officer (England &amp; Wales) is voluntary but is how individual finds end up contributing to the wider archaeological record — worth doing for anything old or unusual. And always agree permission and a finds-sharing arrangement with the landowner before you start.</p>
  </div>
</div>

